Interviewer: Can you briefly describe what you think Gallery Echoes is for, in your own words?

Participant: To me, it’s designed to make visiting a gallery a bit more interactive and reflective. You basically tap the NFC tag next to an artwork to learn more about it, record your own thoughts or feelings, and then you get to see what other visitors noticed or shared about that exact same piece.

Interviewer: Was there any point during the experience where you were unsure what to do next? What happened?

Participant: Most of the flow was actually quite clear, but I did get a little unsure when I reached the “Your moment” page. I saw “Share Feelings” and then “Nearby footprints” right below it, and at first, I wasn’t entirely sure if I was supposed to share my own response first or go explore what other people had posted. I figured it out after looking around for a second, but I think the intended next step could just be slightly clearer.

Interviewer: After tapping the NFC tag, did the steps from viewing the artwork to sharing your feelings make sense to you? Why or why not?

Participant: Yes, overall it definitely made sense. After tapping the tag, I got to see the artwork, the title, the artist, and some background info, which was great. Then there was an option to "add a thought," and after that, the “Your moment” page encouraged me to share my feelings. It felt like a very natural progression from just understanding the artwork to actually reflecting on it. The only thing I might tweak is making the difference between “Add a thought” and “Share Feelings” a bit more obvious, because they do sound quite similar to me.

Interviewer: What did you think the collective visual represented?

Participant: I figured it represented the combined reactions of all the different visitors. I just assumed that things like the colors, shapes, or patterns reflected how people were feeling or responding to that specific piece. I really like the concept, though I think I’d probably need a little explanation or a legend to fully grasp what each visual element actually means.

Interviewer: How did you feel about seeing and interacting with other visitors’ reflections and sketches, such as reading comments or liking sketches?

Participant: I really liked it because it gave me perspectives I probably wouldn’t have caught on my own. Like, seeing comments such as “the bridge almost disappears into the water” or “I noticed the ripples before I saw the lilies”—that actually made me look at the artwork differently. It made the whole gallery feel more social without me having to directly talk to anyone. I’d definitely spend time reading other people’s reflections. I do think the "likes" should stay fairly subtle, though, just so it doesn’t start feeling too much like a typical social media feed.

Interviewer: If you were actually visiting a gallery, which feature would you be most likely to use again, and what would you change or remove?

Participant: I’d definitely use the NFC artwork info and the shared reflections again. I really like being able to quickly learn about a piece and then see what others noticed right after I've formed my own opinion. I’d probably use the “Add a thought” feature too, as long as it was quick. I don't think I would remove any major features, but I would definitely simplify the wording between “Add a thought” and “Share Feelings” since they overlap a bit right now. And like I mentioned before, I’d try to make that collective visual a little easier to interpret.
